// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`agent  help is offline and complete 1`] = `
"Usage: eclipse-code agent <command> [options]

Commands:
  stop             Stop the agent session bound to a canvas node
  delivery         Check retained delivery evidence; never resends input
  history <clear>  History canvas targets
  list             List live agent run states and node titles
  get              Inspect an agent run-state snapshot with its node title
  prompt           Send a prompt to an agent window Eclipse Code launched
  wait             Wait until agents reach a state or finish a turn
  read             Read an agent reply or inspect its current terminal screen

Global options: see "eclipse-code --help".
"
`;

exports[`agent get help is offline and complete 1`] = `
"Usage: eclipse-code agent get --node <id> [options]

Inspect an agent run-state snapshot with its node title

Returns the Worker snapshot plus title and taskNodeId (reverse task-owned link, null if absent/ambiguous), including observed agents inside terminal nodes. A node without registered run state fails with agent.not_found (exit 1); use node get to inspect a terminal/note, or start/resume an agent window. State is runtime evidence, independent of persisted node status. Unknown/degraded is never ready. lastEndedTurn identifies the most recent ended turn with {id, outcome, endedAt} (epoch milliseconds), or null before any end in this incarnation. It persists while the next turn runs and resets on replacement/restart. turnOutcome keeps its legacy behavior: set on end (completed, failed, aborted or unknown), usually cleared on the next start; use lastEndedTurn for identity-bound results.

Options:
  --node <id>         Select a canvas node by its ID; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters) (required)
  --space <selector>  Require every node selector, including full IDs, to belong to this space; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --project <id>      Narrow target lookup to a project; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: nodeId, title, taskNodeId, sessionId, provider, state, blockedReason, blockedSummary, blockedSummary.kind, blockedSummary.questions, turn, turn.id, turn.startedAt, turn.start, lastTransitionAt, source, quality, turnOutcome, lastEndedTurn, lastEndedTurn.id, lastEndedTurn.outcome, lastEndedTurn.endedAt, spawnedBy, lineageDepth, lineageStatus

Field descriptions:
  spawnedBy      ID of the node that created this node; null for a root or unavailable lineage
  lineageDepth   A node started by the user has lineageDepth 1; each agent-created generation adds one. Null when lineage is unavailable
  lineageStatus  recorded when creation is recorded, root when no parent is recorded, unavailable when lineage cannot be read

Projection example:
  eclipse-code agent get --node '<id>' --fields nodeId
  {"nodeId":"node-123"}

Examples:
  eclipse-code agent get --node "Reviewer" --pretty
  eclipse-code agent get --node @self --fields state,quality,turn

Global options: see "eclipse-code --help".
"
`;

exports[`agent list help is offline and complete 1`] = `
"Usage: eclipse-code agent list [options]

List live agent run states and node titles

Returns {epoch, seq, agents}, one row per live agent session. Exited sessions are excluded by agent.list (so --state exited returns no rows); use agent get or wait for a known exited node. taskNodeId is the task whose linkedAgentNodeId points here (null if absent/ambiguous). Titles come from node metadata; null means unbound or deleted. turn.startedAt is epoch milliseconds, or absent when no turn is active. Inside Eclipse Code, the default scope is your space; --project selects a whole project.

When no target is given, inside Eclipse Code defaults to your own space; project options default to your own project.

Shows up to 50 results in Worker order. Use --full or --limit 0 for all; --limit <n> overrides the default.

Options:
  --space <id>                                   Select a space by its ID; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --spawned-by <selector>                        Filter direct children; --project filters results. Creator names use your project, or the selected Space’s project; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --state <working|waiting|idle|exited|unknown>  Filter the authoritative run state
  --full                                         Show all results unless --limit is specified
  --project <id>                                 Narrow target lookup to a project; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

List output: --limit <n> keeps up to n items per list; 0 means all. Cut object lists add top-level truncated totals; bare arrays keep their shape and write one stderr notice. Cut tables also write one stderr notice.

Table output (-o table): supported.
Projectable fields: nodeId, title, taskNodeId, sessionId, provider, state, blockedReason, blockedSummary, blockedSummary.kind, blockedSummary.questions, turn, turn.id, turn.startedAt, turn.start, lastTransitionAt, source, quality, turnOutcome, lastEndedTurn, lastEndedTurn.id, lastEndedTurn.outcome, lastEndedTurn.endedAt, spawnedBy, lineageDepth, lineageStatus

Field descriptions:
  spawnedBy      ID of the node that created this node; null for a root or unavailable lineage
  lineageDepth   A node started by the user has lineageDepth 1; each agent-created generation adds one. Null when lineage is unavailable
  lineageStatus  recorded when creation is recorded, root when no parent is recorded, unavailable when lineage cannot be read

Projection example:
  eclipse-code agent list --fields nodeId
  {"epoch":"e","seq":1,"agents":[{"nodeId":"node-123"}]}

Examples:
  eclipse-code agent list -o table
  eclipse-code agent list --space "Review" --state waiting --fields nodeId,title,state,blockedReason

Global options: see "eclipse-code --help".
"
`;

exports[`agent read help is offline and complete 1`] = `
"Usage: eclipse-code agent read --node <id> [options]

Read an agent reply or inspect its current terminal screen

Read the latest ended reply, or exactly --turn <turnId> from agent prompt. Plain text is the default even when piped; -o json exposes outcome, durability and retained deliveryIds/promptIds. Exact reads never fall back to another turn or transcript.

Loop: prompt → delivery status → wait --turn <turnId> → read --turn <turnId> -o json. Check outcome and reply; never resend because a read fails. Local archived exact reads survive restart. Claude and Codex lifecycle hooks supply exact-turn replies. Codex keeps the first matching Stop reply that ends the local turn; later continuation does not replace it.

--screen shows the visible terminal (last 40 lines); --scrollback adds retained history (last 200; --lines 1–5000; none on the alternate screen). Both work for terminals and agent windows and strip ANSI. Inspect --screen for drafts/dialogs before prompting. Reading never sends input. --screen and --scrollback exclude each other and --last/--turn.

Exit codes: 0 readable result (including empty content); 1 unavailable reply/transcript; 2 invalid input; 4 authentication or grant-policy refusal. Detailed fallback rules, truncation, retention and recovery: eclipse-code skill read.

deliveryIds are retained correlations of Eclipse Code deliveries with this turn, including refused or uncertain attempts. Membership proves neither submission nor consumption, and an empty list proves nothing either: delivery records live in memory only, are cleared when Eclipse Code restarts and expire 30 minutes after resolving; inspect each deliveryId with eclipse-code agent delivery <id>. promptIds are the provider prompt IDs for this turn (Claude continuations included); agent delivery does not accept them.

Options:
  --node <id>         Select a canvas node by its ID; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters) (required)
  --last              Read the latest ended turn (default; --turn selects an exact turn)
  --turn <turnId>     Read exactly this Eclipse Code turn ID from agent prompt; never falls back
  --screen            Inspect the current terminal screen as plain text instead of the final message
  --scrollback        Read retained terminal history and screen (default: 200 lines; maximum: 5000)
  --lines <n>         Return the last n rendered lines (positive integer; requires --screen or --scrollback)
  --space <selector>  Require every node selector, including full IDs, to belong to this space; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --project <id>      Narrow target lookup to a project; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: nodeId, sessionId, turnId, promptId, deliveryIds, promptIds, outcome, persisted, memoryOnlyReason, message, messageKind, truncated, source, provider, startedAt, cwd, resumeSessionId

Projection example:
  eclipse-code agent read --node '<id>' --fields nodeId
  {"nodeId":"node-123"}

Examples:
  eclipse-code agent read --node "Reviewer" --screen
  eclipse-code agent read --node "Shell" --scrollback --lines 200
  eclipse-code agent read --node "Reviewer" --screen --lines 20 -o json
  eclipse-code agent read --node "Reviewer" --last
  eclipse-code agent read --node node-123 --turn turn-123
  eclipse-code agent read --node node-123 --turn turn-123 -o json

Global options: see "eclipse-code --help".
"
`;

exports[`agent wait help is offline and complete 1`] = `
"Usage: eclipse-code agent wait --node <selector> (--turn <turnId> | --until idle|waiting|exited|turn-end [--node <selector> ...] [--any|--all]) [--timeout <duration>] [options]

Wait until agents reach a state or finish a turn

Wait without sending input. Save turnId: prompt → delivery status → wait --turn <turnId> → read --turn <turnId>. Never resend after timeout.

--turn selects one exact turn and includes provider continuations. All ended outcomes exit 0; check outcome. --until selects idle, waiting, exited or a future turn-end. Idle requires no active turn and nondegraded evidence. --all remembers fulfilled bindings; a session change resets progress; --any stops on the first success OR waiting/exited observation of an unfulfilled target.

Exit codes: 0 condition met; 1 unavailable history; 2 invalid input; 4 authentication or grant-policy refusal; 10 waiting observed (the agent may need the user — inspect before acting); 11 target exited before the condition was met (a PTY exit that ends the selected turn succeeds with outcome unknown); 124 timeout; 130 interruption. --through-waiting continues when waiting is reported; it does not suppress 11. Resolve in Eclipse Code, inspect agent read --screen with the same connection options and matching session/turn IDs, then repeat the wait. Prefer --turn: turn-end cannot resume an already observed completion. Details: eclipse-code skill wait.

deliveryIds are retained correlations, including refused or uncertain attempts; membership proves neither submission nor consumption. Records expire 30 minutes after resolving and clear on restart: inspect each deliveryId with eclipse-code agent delivery <id>. promptIds are the provider prompt IDs, not delivery IDs.

Options:
  --node <id>                             Select a canvas node by its ID; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters) (required)
  --until <idle|waiting|exited|turn-end>  Condition to observe
  --turn <turnId>                         Exact Eclipse Code turn ID returned by agent prompt
  --any                                   Stop on the first success or waiting/exited observation of an unfulfilled target
  --all                                   Resolve when all selected nodes match (default)
  --through-waiting                       Continue waiting when a target reports waiting
  --timeout <duration>                    Overall wait budget, e.g. 30s, 2m or 20000ms (default: 20s; minimum: 250ms)
  --space <selector>                      Require every node selector, including full IDs, to belong to this space; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --project <id>                          Narrow target lookup to a project; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

List output: --limit <n> keeps up to n items per list; 0 means all. Cut object lists add top-level truncated totals; bare arrays keep their shape and write one stderr notice. Cut tables also write one stderr notice.

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: nodeId, taskNodeId, sessionId, provider, state, blockedReason, blockedSummary, blockedSummary.kind, blockedSummary.questions, turn, turn.id, turn.startedAt, turn.start, lastTransitionAt, source, quality, turnOutcome, lastEndedTurn, lastEndedTurn.id, lastEndedTurn.outcome, lastEndedTurn.endedAt, completion, completion.nodeId, completion.sessionId, completion.turnId, completion.outcome, endedTurn, endedTurn.id, endedTurn.startedAt, endedTurn.start, endedTurnOutcome, turnId, deliveryIds, promptIds, outcome, endedAt, persisted, memoryOnlyReason

Projection example:
  eclipse-code agent wait --node node-123 --until idle --fields nodeId,state
  {"until":"idle","mode":"all","agents":[{"nodeId":"node-123","state":"idle"}]}

Examples:
  eclipse-code agent wait --node node-123 --turn turn-123 --timeout 2m
  eclipse-code agent wait --node "Reviewer" --until idle --timeout 2m
  eclipse-code agent wait --node node-123 --node node-456 --until waiting --any --timeout 30s

Global options: see "eclipse-code --help".
"
`;

